00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.200
Now back to the show.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.647
Let's start with a quick recap.

00:02:39.647 --> 00:02:42.260
"Elden Ring" takes place in a
world that has fallen apart.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.250
It's rife with creepy monsters,

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.220
sunken churches and dilapidated buildings.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.530
Death and decay are everywhere.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.930
After the Goddess Marika
removed the Death Rune

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:52.870
from the Elden Ring,

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:56.040
assassins responded by
killing several demigods.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.790
- [Woman] On a night of wintry fog,

00:03:00.933 --> 00:03:02.060
(horses thumping)

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.527
the Rune of Death was stolen.

00:03:03.527 --> 00:03:04.740
- Marika then fractured

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.370
the powerful, titular ring into pieces.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.410
Each remaining demigod
grabbed a piece of the ring.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.360
This caused a war that
effectively broke the world,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.480
leaving behind a (beep) dirty wasteland.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.430
Today, shardbearers each
hold a piece of the ring,

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:21.230
and if you can simply unite them,

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:23.910
it seems you can return
the land to prosperity.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.460
To better understand your quest,

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.430
it helps to look to Jacques Derrida

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:30.010
and his concept of hauntology,

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.320
coined in the 1993 book
"Specters of Marx."

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.000
Here's the deal, Derrida said
that the present is marked

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.290
by a temporal disjunction

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.280
in which history seems to break

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.510
and a certain narrative of
the past haunts the present,

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:44.620
like a ghost.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.920
Derrida was responding
to what the West saw

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.270
as the death of communism after
the fall of the Berlin Wall.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.630
But he argues that Marxism never died.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.830
Rather, it lived on as a specter

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.350
continuing to haunt Western society.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.550
For some, that specter is frightening,

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.250
a looming threat with the potential

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:02.920
to destroy Western capitalism.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.330
For others, that specter is mournful,

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.450
because they view Marxism as
a ghost of lost potential,

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.190
a specter of a world that could have been.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.480
And the future that could have been

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.530
remains a ghostly presence,

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.660
gone but never forgotten.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.630
Aspects of the Marxist dream
are seen in fits and starts,

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:23.490
but never with the same momentum
or clarity as in the past.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.630
Like when Bernie Sanders ran
a presidential campaign that,

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.720
while definitely not Marxist,

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.320
was based on dismantling
certain structures

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.870
of neoliberal capitalism

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.430
in order to facilitate greater equality.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:38.600
We see hauntology alive
and well in America

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.210
in the lingering nostalgia for the 1950s,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.030
a time supposedly defined
by happy housewives,

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.200
a booming economy, affordable housing,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.640
and college education for all.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.640
White picket fences and
"Leave It to Beaver"

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.890
seem infinitely more attractive

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:55.630
than what the present has to offer.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:57.430
The specter of the '50s, thus,

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.370
leaves us longing for
America's glory days.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:03.020
We see this same mythologizing
throughout "Elden Ring."

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.950
It's a world defined by its destruction.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.520
But even the most tragic
ruins you encounter

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.080
seem to whisper faint
memories of past glory.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.780
Indeed, this was even embedded
in the development process.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:16.740
George R.R. Martin was recruited

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.480
to build the world of "Elden
Ring's" glorious past.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:22.920
Then, Miyazaki and co. brought
that world to present day

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.260
and turned it nasty.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.270
A castle with collapsed towers

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:29.270
evokes halcyon days of
flying banners and pageantry.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:32.340
There's even a secret boss
fight where time rewinds

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.080
and a dragon returns to life

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.350
to reassert its power and glory.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:40.110
Remnants of an ancient,
glorious past are everywhere

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.870
in this un-glorious, unwelcoming world.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:44.780
And also literal ghosts.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.200
Lots of ghosts haunting us in the present.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.230
So, telling stories about
how great the past was

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:51.890
is all well and good.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:53.450
But as with most storytelling,

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:55.660
narratives about the past
are typically riddled

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.670
with nostalgic half-truths, outright lies,

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.150
and everything in between.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.660
So when society creates a
fabricated version of the past,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.030
what happens to the real
past that actually existed?

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:08.530
According to Derrida,

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:11.140
the real, less-savory version of the past

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.690
sort of peeks through society's curtains,

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:17.110
influencing our world in
subtle and not-subtle ways.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:20.040
This is where we get the
haunting part of hauntology,

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.060
as the real lived past becomes
the greatest ghost of all,

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.630
a looming specter haunting the present.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:29.470
We see this in the real
version of the 1950s

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.420
that continues to haunt America.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:32.710
Racism was rampant

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:35.760
and the redlining that occurred
continues to be realized

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.530
in unequal housing
opportunities to this day.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.860
The decade also saw a major step backwards

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.280
for women in the workplace,

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.870
which continues to be seen in a wage gap

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.640
that is alive and well.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.130
Everything from the Red Scare

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:50.380
to the genuine fear of nuclear war

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.160
to American foreign policy decisions

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.450
that wreaked havoc on South America,

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:55.920
among other places,

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.970
it all gets erased when we reminisce

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:59.540
about America's glory days.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:01.300
Still, many of us living

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.480
in an objectively unappealing present

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:06.470
look back on this imagined
past with nostalgia.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:08.810
The world of "Elden Ring," like America,

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.770
has a narrativized past
defined by greatness.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:15.160
But, similarly, the real
past haunts the present

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.690
and suggests something more nefarious.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:20.360
And perhaps the scariest
ghosts in "Elden Ring"

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.580
aren't the ones lurking in creepy manors,

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:25.710
but the world's real, yet unspoken, past.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.670
There are plenty of signs indicating

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.900
that the olden days were not as glorious

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:30.930
as everyone tells you.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.330
Hell, if it had been so
awesome, there would be no game.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:37.500
What's more, there doesn't
seem to be a consistent vision

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.070
of what the glorious past actually was.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:42.390
The enemies you fight
are people and creatures

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:43.780
with opposing motivations,

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.810
suggesting that the old
world wasn't so peaceful.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.520
In fact, it seems to have been rife

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:51.150
with factions and organizations
competing for power.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:53.063
- [Man] Praetor Rykard.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.473
Lunar Princess Ranni.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:05.660
Wilful traitors, all.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:06.610
- [Michael] It's also hinted

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.250
that the legend of the Golden Order,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.960
supposedly supported by an ineffable god,

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.870
has been given a glossy varnish.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.810
- [Man] The rhythms and
calculus of the master's finger

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:20.030
betray a suspicion of the
holism of the Golden Order.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:22.430
- [Michael] After all, if
everybody got along so well,

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.860
why did things descend into war?

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.380
And if the world was so perfect,

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.300
why did Marika decide to break it?

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.890
All signs indicate that this perfect world

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:32.210
may have been more illusory

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.253
than we're initially led to believe.

00:08:34.253 --> 00:08:36.580
- How long will it be, I wonder,

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.530
before the Tarnished tire of
obeisance to the Two Fingers?

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:42.750
- After all, not everyone seems so big

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:44.330
on the whole Golden Order thing.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:47.060
Plenty of characters are
determined to destroy it

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:48.480
by burning the Erdtree.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.230
Sure, some of those
characters are horrifying,

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.660
like this snake man.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:57.660
- Together, we will devour the very gods.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:00.340
- [Michael] But some seem genuinely good,

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.820
like the maiden who
guides you on your quest.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.160
She even entreats you
to burn down the tree.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:10.160
- So I'd like you to undertake
a new journey, with me.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.850
To the flame of ruin,

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.310
far above the clouds,

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:19.200
upon the snowy mountaintops of the giants.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.490
Then I can set the Erdtree aflame.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:26.590
- [Michael] An act deemed
the cardinal sin by others.

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:31.590
- The burning of the Erdtree
is the first cardinal sin.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:34.310
- [Michael] All of which
suggests that good and evil

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:35.980
might not be so simple here.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:38.040
The idea of being haunted by the past,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.310
or some imagined version of it,

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:41.970
might not be too surprising.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.700
But when writer Mark Fisher
expanded on the idea,

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:47.550
he identified something
arguably more disturbing.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.970
When you're haunted by an imagined past,

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.350
you're also haunted by what
Fisher calls lost futures,

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:56.800
i.e. potential futures that
could have materialized

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:58.580
if not for the events in the past

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:00.360
that closed those paths off.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.827
He cites philosopher Franco
Berardi who coined the phrase,

00:10:03.827 --> 00:10:06.430
"the slow cancellation of the future."

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:08.110
This describes his view of the way

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:10.640
any hope for a happy future in the West

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.220
was eroded from the late
'70s into the 21st century,

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.630
as the rise of neoliberalism
attacked social welfare,

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:20.810
lionized consumerism, wreaked
havoc on the environment,

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:21.643
and so on.

00:10:21.643 --> 00:10:22.476
As Fisher puts it,

00:10:22.476 --> 00:10:24.697
"The slow cancellation of the future

00:10:24.697 --> 00:10:27.787
"has been accompanied by a
deflation of expectations.

00:10:27.787 --> 00:10:29.447
"The feeling of belatedness,

00:10:29.447 --> 00:10:31.377
"of living after the gold rush,

00:10:31.377 --> 00:10:34.340
"is as omnipresent as it is disavowed."

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.210
The sense of futurelessness
in our own society

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:40.050
can best be realized by looking
to the comparative optimism

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.200
of the 1950s.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.750
Back then, visions of the
future were starry-eyed,

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:45.230
just think of "The Jetsons."

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:47.140
Yet in our contemporary world,

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:49.720
this vision no longer seems attainable.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:52.070
The vision of the future
as imagined in the '50s

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:53.660
can be considered lost,

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:56.770
yet it continues to pervade our culture

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.870
in the form of retrofuturism,

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:02.220
i.e. images in culture,
technology, and so on

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:03.920
that evoke what people in the '50s

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:05.850
thought the future would look like.

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:08.627
Retrofuturism in culture
continues to perpetuate

00:11:08.627 --> 00:11:10.360
"The Jetsons" vision of the future,

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.170
even as that path now seems impossible.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:15.360
Such is the power of ghosts.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.620
We see this, too, throughout "Elden Ring,"

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:20.790
where characters wax poetic
about what could have been

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.690
had the world not been destroyed.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:25.940
You have nobles asking you to
recapture their lost castles,

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.200
daughters asking for help
finding their father,

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:29.920
even a weird little man

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.510
who just wants to live in his cave again.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:34.260
Take the demigod Radahn,

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:35.850
who was, before the shattering,

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:39.070
a powerful warrior and
servant of the Golden Order.

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.370
During the war he was
infected with a disease

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:42.590
and driven mad.

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:45.330
In the midst of the broken
world of The Lands Between,

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:46.880
he holds tournaments at a castle

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:50.010
where warriors can fight
him for the ultimate prize,

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:52.338
his great rune of the Elden Ring.

00:11:52.338 --> 00:11:55.420
- [Man] The stars have aligned.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.633
The festival is nigh.

00:11:58.773 --> 00:12:02.490
General Radahn, mightiest demigod

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.920
of the Shattering, awaits you.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.130
- Clearly, he can't let go of his time

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:08.550
as a warrior and ruler,

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.890
instead continuing to fight,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.830
trying to relive his glory days.

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:14.820
In fact, his fellow soldiers from the war

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.380
are continuing to haphazardly fight,

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:21.110
though there's no longer any
real battlefield to fight on.

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:24.300
Clearly, Radahn is in
mourning for the lost future

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.620
where he would have
maintained his greatness.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:27.670
So much is his grief

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.580
that he even uses his
powerful gravity magic

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.920
to hold the stars in place.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.200
This keeps the world in stasis,

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:38.070
hiding from a future where
he actually has to confront

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.600
the loss and destruction
of the broken Elden Ring,

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.950
and only once you defeat him
can the stars move again.

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:47.730
So, clearly, your quest
within "Elden Ring"

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.860
isn't just to resurrect a lost past

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:51.370
by restoring the Golden Order.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.600
It's much bigger than that.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:55.530
By putting the pieces of the ring together

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:56.930
and restoring that past,

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:59.160
you're also reclaiming a lost future

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.700
as an extension of that glorious past.

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.690
But who benefits from this?

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:05.930
Well, most of the characters urging you on

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:09.100
are servants or beneficiaries
of the Golden Order.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.660
That includes both heroes and villains

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:14.010
who want to return to a past they believe

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:15.380
was taken from them.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:18.070
But the various endings,
which we'll detail more later,

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:20.870
call the nobleness of
this act into question.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:23.000
Like many FromSoftware games,

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:25.410
the majority of endings
have you reconstructing

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:27.020
a version of the status quo

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:30.570
and instantiating a hollow
version of this lost future.

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:34.070
Now, underpinning the idea
of a world of lost futures

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:36.620
is a sense that we can no longer imagine

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:38.930
any kind of actually attainable future.

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:42.350
The lost futures that haunt
us simply loom too large.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:44.760
Fisher identifies this lack of imagination

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.970
in his book "Capitalist Realism,"

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:49.487
a term he defines as "the widespread sense

00:13:49.487 --> 00:13:52.247
"that not only is
capitalism the only viable

00:13:52.247 --> 00:13:54.017
"political and economic system,

00:13:54.017 --> 00:13:57.187
"but also that it is now
impossible even to imagine

00:13:57.187 --> 00:13:58.940
"a coherent alternative to it."

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:00.090
Though the world of "Elden Ring"

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.500
has nothing to do with capitalism,

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:04.110
we do see a similar vibe,

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:06.760
one of not being able to
imagine an alternative

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.700
to the world in which you're living.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.230
Take the demigod Renalla.

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:13.800
She's trapped in a library
surrounded by her creepy children

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.030
and holding what appears
to be a squishy, amber egg.

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:17.863
The game hints

00:14:17.863 --> 00:14:20.490
that after Renalla's daughter
Ranni killed herself,

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:24.012
Renalla became obsessed
with this egg of rebirth.

00:14:24.012 --> 00:14:27.227
- [Renalla] Oh little
Ranni, my dear daughter.

00:14:30.468 --> 00:14:32.718
Weave thy night into being.

00:14:33.556 --> 00:14:35.220
- [Michael] Rather than
move beyond her loss

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:36.360
into a new future,

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.150
she is consumed by a desire

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:40.550
to recreate the daughter that she lost.

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:43.530
She literally can't imagine an alternative

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.040
to her current situation.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:49.500
- [Renalla] Dither not, come out say I.

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:52.530
Or will ye be gravestones?

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.150
To be better born anew?

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:56.880
- Fisher believed that this stasis

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.737
makes sense under neoliberalism,

00:14:58.737 --> 00:15:02.787
"a system that is unresponsive,
impersonal, centreless,

00:15:02.787 --> 00:15:04.490
"abstract and fragmentary."

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:07.120
In a world so subsumed by capitalism,

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.640
a world that imagines every
person as primarily a consumer,

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:13.820
every thing as primarily
a product to be consumed,

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:17.790
the prospect of change feels
kind of hollow and hopeless.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:18.737
As he puts it,

00:15:18.737 --> 00:15:20.867
"Capitalism seamlessly occupies

00:15:20.867 --> 00:15:22.660
"the horizons of the thinkable."

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:25.670
Fisher often identifies this
capitalist realist sense

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:27.380
that there is no future

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:28.770
in our art and culture.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.030
He sees the endless postmodern culture

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:34.300
of remixing and remaking,
of pastiche and nostalgia,

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.350
as evidence that we have lost the capacity

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:39.860
to imagine anything that has
not already been created.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:43.130
This dooms us to repeat
the structures of the past,

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:45.070
both in art and in life.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:46.957
Even the counterculture today, he argues,

00:15:46.957 --> 00:15:48.897
"endlessly repeat older gestures

00:15:48.897 --> 00:15:50.787
"of rebellion and contestation

00:15:50.787 --> 00:15:52.790
"as if for the first time."

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.480
And the concept of a
society stuck on a loop

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:58.330
pervades "Elden Ring"
far more than you'd see

00:15:58.330 --> 00:15:59.720
in an average video game.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:02.530
The gameplay itself is
remarkably repetitive,

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:05.870
which is probably a good
thing given how hard it is.

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:08.890
In fact, it's basically
defined by repetition,

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:11.080
particularly with regards to death.

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:14.283
First off, you die, like, a lot.

00:16:14.283 --> 00:16:17.224
(woman grunting)

00:16:17.224 --> 00:16:19.186
(woman screams)

00:16:19.186 --> 00:16:20.236
- [Man] Oh no. Me no.

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:23.290
- But what's more, when you die,

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:25.550
it's not like in, say, "Mario,"

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:27.700
where you just reset and try again.

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:29.180
Rather, when you die,

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:31.430
the runes you were carrying are lost

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:33.580
unless you go back and retrieve them.

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:35.960
This forces you to
return to sites of defeat

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:37.920
over and over again

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.250
with different approaches or better plans

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:42.110
in order to preserve your progress

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:44.860
and grow as both a player and a character.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.880
Each time you know a little
better what to expect

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:51.600
and possibly how to
avoid yet another death.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:54.610
And that brings us to the
first not depressing part

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:55.550
of this video,

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:59.020
the possible emancipatory
potential of hauntology,

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:01.120
and specifically, of repetition.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.060
See, being haunted by lost futures

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:05.660
doesn't doom us to a life of what ifs.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:07.510
At least, not necessarily.

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:10.750
Fisher also believed that these
ghosts can also inspire us

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:12.980
to see different potentials for the world

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:14.400
outside of our present.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:16.450
Maybe that means imagining a world

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:19.140
where we don't have to
rely on fossil fuels

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.210
that are slowly suffocating the planet,

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:22.630
or where you don't have to view yourself

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.750
as a sentient personal brand.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:26.330
Back in the 1960s,

00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:28.410
philosopher Gilles Deleuze recognized

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:29.780
the potential of repetition

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:32.960
as an "active force producing difference."

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:36.147
He saw repetition as
"the power of beginning

00:17:36.147 --> 00:17:37.460
"and beginning again."

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:39.520
He was responding in
part to Sigmund Freud,

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:42.870
who saw repetition as a
psychological compulsion,

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:44.800
a need to recreate a past

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:46.830
that feels safe in its familiarity.

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:50.330
Conversely, Deleuze, inspired
by Nietzsche and Kierkegaard,

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:53.410
understood repetition as a
springboard for reinvention,

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:55.170
for conceiving of new choices,

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:57.680
for creating spaces for difference.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:00.860
We see this idea of
repetition as reinvention

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:03.710
at the core of the
game, in its conclusion.

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:06.830
Now, most "Souls" games
are pretty depressing.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:09.140
They often underscore
the futility of trying,

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:11.900
the false illusion of choice in a universe

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.020
where most things are predetermined.

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.800
But not "Elden Ring."

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:17.350
At least, not every time.

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:19.830
Notably, the game offers several endings.

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:22.410
In one, everybody dies. Bummer.

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:24.480
In most, you restore the world

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:26.140
pretty close to how it used to be,

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:27.970
but always with a wrinkle.

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:31.200
There's also one where you
burn literally everything down

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.290
in a frenzy of destruction.

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:36.360
But there's one ending that
we find really interesting.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:38.680
In it, you restore the Elden Ring,

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:40.780
but rather than handing it back to Marika

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:42.530
and returning to the Golden Order,

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:44.470
you give that power to Ranni.

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:47.050
Remember, the daughter who died by suicide

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:49.610
but is now back as a
ghost who lives in a doll.

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:53.010
Like we've said, this game
really is all about haunting.

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:56.790
- [Woman] Oh? A dogged fellow, aren't we?

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.770
Or is it merely thy
habit to talk to dolls?

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:01.210
- With this newfound power,

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.610
Ranni changes the nature
of the Golden Order,

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:05.740
which was once a God-like force

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:09.090
that exerted power on and
intervened in the world.

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:10.370
Under Ranni's rules,

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:13.630
the Golden Order is still a
system that, like gravity,

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:15.270
sets certain rules.

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:18.020
But now the people
within it have free will

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:21.640
and can abide by or violate
its norms as they see fit.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:24.830
In this way, a world once
ruled by predestination

00:19:24.830 --> 00:19:28.560
is now ruled by the uncertainty
that is individual choice.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.260
And choice, as basically

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.640
every existentialist
philosopher would tell you,

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.420
is a necessary prerequisite for freedom.

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:37.870
In this way, Ranni has
imagined a different world,

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:40.410
one where visions of the
future are not dictated

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:41.830
by myths of the past.

00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:43.620
And according to folks like Mark Fisher,

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:45.970
that's what our real world needs most.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.690
People willing to conceive
of new possibilities,

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:50.650
rather than passively accepting

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:53.460
all the logical
inevitabilities of our present.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:55.900
Of course, if you want
to reinvent the future,

00:19:55.900 --> 00:19:59.470
you have to let go of the
mythologized glorious past

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:00.890
you were trying to restore

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:03.727
and accept the past as it actually was.

00:20:03.727 --> 00:20:06.380
That's the only way to
stop being haunted by it

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:08.280
and to realize a different future,

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:11.670
whether it's in a video
game or in the real world.

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:13.010
But what do you guys think?

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:15.720
Is "Elden Ring" a game about
letting go of the past,

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:18.320
or are our brains just broken

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.190
from trying to understand Derrida?

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:22.210
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.490
A huge thanks to all of our patrons,

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:27.170
and if you haven't checked
out our Patreon recently,

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:29.950
we have a lot of very
cool new stuff coming.

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:32.380
And not in the imagined
future sort of way,

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:34.040
it's all really happening.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:36.440
So please check out our Patreon page,

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:38.620
I think you're gonna like what's going on.

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:40.190
Set fire to that subscribe button

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:42.160
like you've got a dragon head for a hand,

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:43.800
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:46.344
And as always, thanks for watching, later.

00:20:46.344 --> 00:20:48.927
(upbeat music)

